# Line endings, decided here rather than per-machine.
#
# Git normalises text to LF when it stores a file, and converts it back on checkout
# using whatever core.autocrlf happens to be set to on the machine doing the checkout.
# That is fine until the machine is somebody else's. Committing from Windows stored
# build_windows.bat as LF; the Mac build runners checked it back out as LF; and a .bat
# with Unix line endings is unreliable in cmd.exe around the multi-line
# `if errorlevel ( ... )` blocks that script depends on to stop after a failed test.
#
# It would also have gone out that way inside the "Source code (zip)" that GitHub
# attaches to every release — so this was not only a test failing, it was the shipped
# build script being subtly wrong for anybody who used it.
#
# The eol= settings below override core.autocrlf on every platform, so each file is
# checked out with the endings it actually needs no matter who is checking it out.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows reads these. They need CRLF wherever they land.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

# And these are read by a Unix shell, where a CRLF breaks the shebang line and the
# error is "bad interpreter", which names nothing useful.
*.sh       text eol=lf
*.command  text eol=lf

# Never rewritten. text=auto normally works this out on its own; saying so explicitly
# means it cannot get it wrong on a file added later.
*.png   binary
*.ico   binary
*.icns  binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.db    binary
